{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of quiet that only exists in kitchens at 2 a.m.\n\nIt's not an empty quiet. It's full. The refrigerator hums like a low, tired monk. The faucet ticks once every sixteen seconds from a leak you keep meaning to fix. Outside, the streetlight throws orange squares onto your linoleum.\n\nI used to hate this hour. In my twenties, it felt like an accusation. Everyone else was asleep, living correctly, while I was awake, failing at rest. Now I'm forty-one, and I understand it differently.\n\nThe world finally stops asking you for things. No emails, no pings, no one needing a decision from you about dinner or deadlines. The quiet isn't judging you. It's keeping you company.\n\nI sit at the table with cold tea I forgot to drink hours ago, and I don't feel lonely. I feel accompanied by all the other people awake in other kitchens, in other time zones, sitting with their own cold tea, listening to their own refrigerator monk.\n\nWe are a secret, sleepless congregation. And for a little while, that is enough.\n\nTomorrow morning the house will be loud again. The kettle will scream, the news will chatter, someone will ask where the socks are. I will be tired. But I will carry a little piece of this 2 a.m. stillness folded in my pocket, like a smooth stone, to touch when the day gets too sharp.\n\nThat is the real purpose of staying up late, I think.",
